urkish  immigrant  who made his family life here in uh  in germany and the daughter very young  daughter of a man  who was very young when he died but did  not die of covert 19 he died of leukemia  of cancer of the blood  but also died basically on his own  alone because of the restrictions and  finally there was  the son of a woman who was in a care  home because she was  suffering from dementia and she too was  in a sense left alone because her family  was not able  to see her during that time is there a  reason  for the timing of this service why now  there is no exact reason to there has  been some debate about this in fact some  people have said the commemoration  should have happened a lot earlier  other countries have had similar events  before  some people say we should be  commemorating victims continuously  because people continue to die and we  should not ignore that  the president himself has said it's now  been a year or just over a year since  the pandemic struck  we cannot wait for the pandemic to end  to do this commemoration because we  don't know when it will end  and so he's saying it's a moment to  stand back  and to in a sense uh commemorate but  also to gather our strength  for the new continuing infections the  continuing the third wave as we're  talking about it at the moment  of this pandemic now uh sticking to that  uh looking ahead germany as you rightly  said it's in a grip of a third wave with  record new infections in recent days  what is the government proposing to do  there's been a lot of dispute about what  is the right way to go  and this dispute has been in fact going  on for months mainly it's  the central government of angela merkel  the chancellor on the one hand  and on the other side the 16 federal  states and their leaders  who in fact have um the the power to  decide what happens in their regions  and while angela merkel has always been  for stricter measures  the regional leaders have resisted that  and that continues to t